      "body": "## Motivation\n\nDeepGEMM raises `AssertionError` during import when `find_cuda_home()` cannot locate a CUDA toolkit. Both availability probes currently let this escape into model imports, leaving the model registry empty even for architectures such as Qwen2 that do not require DeepGEMM.\n\nRefs #48080. This addresses the optional-import failure only; it does not change registry loading or startup performance.\n\n## Modifications\n\n- Treat import-time `AssertionError` as an unavailable optional dependency in both the SM120/121 API probe and the general DeepGEMM probe.\n- Add CPU regressions covering the failing import and a working installation, with mocked SM90/100/120/121 detection.\n\n## Accuracy Tests\n\n- **14 targeted CPU tests passed:** the new availability tests, existing DeepGEMM UE8M0 requantization tests, and model-registry tests. The new regression fails on unmodified `40a64e1b` for all four SM cases.\n- **H100, `sgl-deep-gemm==0.2.5.post3`:** unsetting CUDA paths, removing `nvcc` from `PATH`, and hiding `/usr/local/cuda` reproduced the actual DeepGEMM assertion. Registry entries changed from **0 before the fix to 231 after**, including Qwen2 (292 with the toolkit available).\n- **Separate fallback smoke test:** with the toolkit available for other kernels, injecting a DeepGEMM import assertion still allowed `Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct` BF16 inference. Two greedy generations matched the normal-installation outputs. Used Triton attention, PyTorch sampling, and the Python radix-tree backend.\n- **Positive control:** DeepGEMM remained enabled with a working installation; its BF16 GEMM matched PyTorch on H100.\n\nThe fix does not make inference toolkit-free: a cold Qwen2 launch without the toolkit still needs NVCC to compile its RoPE kernel. Models that directly require DeepGEMM also remain subject to that dependency.\n\n## Speed Tests and Profiling\n\nNot applicable: only optional-dependency import error handling changes.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Changed-file pre-commit checks passed. A full `pre-commit run --all-files` passed all non-Rust hooks; the four Rust hooks could not execute because the local machine lacks `cargo`/`rustup`.\n- [x] Added registered CPU regression coverage.\n- [x] Followed the repository code style and test layout.\n- [x] Documented validation and its limits above; no user-facing API or documentation change.\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:x: [Run #41618409418](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/41618409418)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #41618408981](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/41618408981)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 10): <!-- slot:pr-test-amd:start -->:x: [Run #41618408990](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/41618408990)<!-- slot:pr-test-amd:end -->\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Base CI only covers the core cases, and its workload may not exercise your change. Use `/rerun-test` to run the tests your change touches, or copy this prompt for an agent:\n>\n> ```text\n> Find the registered tests this PR affects and run them with /rerun-test, following https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/blob/main/.agents/skills/rerun-test/SKILL.md\n> ```\n<!-- pr-states:end -->",
